-- title:
--   Descent orbits of relative Pic⁰ points lie in affine charts
-- statement:
--   Let $R$ be a commutative ring, $c\colon C\to\operatorname{Spec}R$ a separated morphism and $\varepsilon$ a section of $c$ (a morphism over the identity of $\operatorname{Spec}R$), and let $R'$ be an $R$-algebra that is module-finite, étale and faithfully flat. Write $C_{R'}$ for `SmoothProperCurve.baseChange R c R'`, the second projection of the pullback of $c$ along $\operatorname{Spec}R'\to\operatorname{Spec}R$, with induced section $\varepsilon_{R'}$. Let $D'$ consist of a scheme $D'.P$, a morphism $D'.\mathrm{toBase}\colon D'.P\to\operatorname{Spec}R'$ and a section of it, and let $h'$ be data exhibiting $D'$ as representing the subfunctor of the rigidified relative Picard presheaf of $(C_{R'},\varepsilon_{R'})$ cut out by `algEquivZeroCut`, i.e. by the condition that a rigidified line bundle be algebraically equivalent to zero on every geometric fibre in the sense of `IsAlgEquivZero`; $h'$ supplies a Poincaré bundle satisfying the condition, the universal property (unique classifying morphism over the base for each such bundle, up to isomorphism of the underlying modules) and triviality along the zero section. Assume $D'.\mathrm{toBase}$ is locally of finite type. Blocks are given by $M\in\mathbb N$, $R$-algebras $B_i$ ($i\in\operatorname{Fin}M$) and morphisms $z_i\colon\operatorname{Spec}B_i\to C$ over $\operatorname{Spec}R$; for each $i$ there are $\deg i\ge 1$ sections $\sigma_{i,m}$ of $C_{R'}$ over $\operatorname{Spec}R'$, each of which factors through $z_i$ after composition with the first projection, via some $\operatorname{Spec}R'\to\operatorname{Spec}B_i$. Fix $r,g\in\mathbb N$, an index type $\iota$ and an indexing $\mathrm{idx}$ of $\iota$ by pairs consisting of an injective tuple $a\colon\operatorname{Fin}(r-g)\to\operatorname{Fin}M$ and a labelling $m$ choosing, for each $i$, an element of $\operatorname{Fin}(\deg i)$. Charts are schemes $X_i$ ($i\in\iota$) with morphisms $f_i$ from the (lifted) presheaf represented by $X_i$ to the total presheaf `overTotal` of the above subfunctor, each $f_i$ being an open immersion in the sense of `MorphismProperty.presheafULift` applied to `IsOpenImmersion`, and each $X_i$ having the property that every finite subset is contained in an affine open. Further data are relative effective Cartier divisors $D_\gamma(i)$ of degree $r-g$ on $C_{R'}$ over the identity of $\operatorname{Spec}R'$ such that, for $\mathrm{idx}(a,m)$, the ideal sheaf of $D_\gamma$ is the product over $j$ of the kernel ideals of the graphs of the sections $\sigma_{a_j,m_{a_j}}$. The invertibility and base-change hypotheses are: the ideal sheaf of the rigidifying section is invertible (locally generated by a non-zero-divisor on affine basic opens) over every base $t\colon T\to\operatorname{Spec}R'$; the $r$-th twist `sectionTwist` (the dual of the $r$-th power of that ideal) is compatible with pullback along every morphism of bases; and each pullback of $D_\gamma(i)$ to an arbitrary base has invertible ideal sheaf. The membership criterion `hmem` states that, for each $i$, each point $x$ of the total presheaf on a test scheme $T$ whose structure morphism to $\operatorname{Spec}R'$ is locally of finite type, each rigidified line bundle $L$ representing the class component of $x$, and each field-valued point $s\colon\operatorname{Spec}k\to T$, the vanishing (as `Subsingleton`) of the two-chart Čech $H^1$ of the fibre at $s$ of $L\otimes(\text{section twist by }r)\otimes(\text{ideal module of the pullback of }D_\gamma(i))$, for every two-affine open cover of the fibre product, implies that $s$ lifts through $f_i$ to a morphism $\operatorname{Spec}k\to X_i$ compatible with $x$. The general-position hypothesis `hgp` states that for every algebraically closed field $\Omega$ that is an $R$-algebra and every invertible module $L_0$ on $C_\Omega$ which is algebraically equivalent to zero there is an injective tuple $a\colon\operatorname{Fin}(r-g)\to\operatorname{Fin}M$ such that, for every family of $\Omega$-sections $v_j$ of $C_\Omega$ with each $v_j$ factoring through $z_{a_j}$ via an $R$-algebra map $B_{a_j}\to\Omega$, the two-chart Čech $H^1$ of $L_0$ tensored with the dual of the $r$-th power of the ideal of the section point of $\varepsilon$ and with the module of the product of the ideals of the $v_j$ vanishes for every two-affine open cover. The conclusion is that for every point $x$ of $D'.P$ there is an affine open $W\subseteq D'.P$ such that every point $y$ of the pullback of $D'.\mathrm{toBase}$ followed by $\operatorname{Spec}R'\to\operatorname{Spec}R$ against $\operatorname{Spec}R'\to\operatorname{Spec}R$ with first projection $x$ satisfies that the image of $y$ under the action morphism of the descent action `DescentAction.ofRepresentableBy`, obtained from the representability of the restricted presheaf `relSubPicPresheaf c ε (algEquivZeroCut c ε)` by $D'.\mathrm{toBase}$ via `representableByRestrict`, lies in $W$.
--
--   This is the affine-locality condition required for effective descent of the relative $\mathrm{Pic}^0$ scheme from $\operatorname{Spec}R'$ to $\operatorname{Spec}R$: the orbit of a point of $D'.P$ under the canonical descent action attached to the finite étale cover is contained in a single affine open. It is used in the construction of a representing scheme for the algebraic-equivalence-to-zero cut of the relative Picard presheaf over the base, via the chart system indexed by injective tuples of blocks.
